window.docContents[191].resolve({"/Maps/#section-embeddings-and-maps-of-mycielskians":{"contents":"There is a natural embedding of a graph G into its Mycielskian mμ[G], given\nby sending a vertex v to the left copy of v in the Mycielskian.\n\nThe graph embedding G ↪g mμ[G] of a graph into its Mycielskian./-- The graph embedding G ↪g mμ[G] of a graph into its Mycielskian. -/\n@[simps]\ndef Embedding.mycielskianLeft {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) : G ↪g mμ[G] where\n  toFun := (·ₗ) -- V → Option (V ⊕ V); v ↦ some (Sum.inl v)\n  inj' := Option.some_injective _ |>.comp Sum.inl_injective\n  map_rel_iff' := Iff.rfl\n\n\nAny graph homomorphism f : G →g H induces a homomorphism mμ[G] →g mμ[H]. Notice\nthat below we use some useful shorthand for defining a function on Option (V ⊕ V).\n\n#check Option.elim'\n#check Sum.elim\n\n\nA graph homomorphism f : G →g H induces a homomorphism mμ[G] →g mμ[H],\nshowing that the Mycielskian is functorial on SimpleGraph.Hom./-- A graph homomorphism f : G →g H induces a homomorphism mμ[G] →g mμ[H],\nshowing that the Mycielskian is functorial on SimpleGraph.Hom. -/\n@[simps]\ndef Hom.mapMycielskian {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    (f : G →g H) : mμ[G] →g mμ[H] where\n  toFun := Option.elim' none <| Sum.elim (fun v => (f v)ₗ) (fun v => (f v)ᵣ)\n  map_rel' := by\n    intro u v h\n    rcases u with _ | (_|_) <;> rcases v with _ | (_|_) <;>\n      simp_all [f.map_adj]\n\n\nHom.mapMycielskian sends the identity homomorphism on G to the identity on\nmμ[G]./-- Hom.mapMycielskian sends the identity homomorphism on G to the identity on\nmμ[G]. -/\ntheorem Hom.mapMycielskian_id {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) :\n    (Hom.id (G := G)).mapMycielskian = Hom.id := by\n  ext x; rcases x with _ | (_|_) <;> rfl\n\n\nHom.mapMycielskian distributes over composition: the map induced by g.comp f is\nthe composite of the maps induced by f and g. (Together with\nHom.mapMycielskian_id this says that the Mycielskian is a functor on the category\nof simple graphs and homomorphisms: it acts on homomorphisms as well as on graphs,\ncompatibly with identities and composition.)\n\nlemma Hom.mapMycielskian_comp {V W X : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n    {H : SimpleGraph W} {K : SimpleGraph X} (f : G →g H) (g : H →g K) :\n    (g.comp f).mapMycielskian = g.mapMycielskian.comp f.mapMycielskian := by\n  ext x; rcases x with _ | (_|_) <;> rfl\n\n\nThe same construction turns embeddings into embeddings and isomorphisms into\nisomorphisms.\n\nA graph embedding f : G ↪g H induces an embedding mμ[G] ↪g mμ[H]./-- A graph embedding f : G ↪g H induces an embedding mμ[G] ↪g mμ[H]. -/\n@[simps]\ndef Embedding.mapMycielskian {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    (f : G ↪g H) : mμ[G] ↪g mμ[H] where\n  toFun := f.toHom.mapMycielskian\n  inj' u v h := by\n    rcases u with _ | (_|_) <;> rcases v with _ | (_|_) <;> simp_all\n  map_rel_iff' {u v} := by\n    rcases u with _ | (_|_) <;> rcases v with _ | (_|_) <;> simp [Hom.mapMycielskian]\n\n\nA graph isomorphism f : G ≃g H induces an isomorphism mμ[G] ≃g mμ[H]./-- A graph isomorphism f : G ≃g H induces an isomorphism mμ[G] ≃g mμ[H]. -/\n@[simps]\ndef Iso.mapMycielskian {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    (f : G ≃g H) : mμ[G] ≃g mμ[H] where\n  toFun  := f.toHom.mapMycielskian\n  invFun := f.symm.toHom.mapMycielskian\n  left_inv x := by rcases x with _ | (_|_) <;> simp\n  right_inv x := by rcases x with _ | (_|_) <;> simp\n  map_rel_iff' := f.toEmbedding.mapMycielskian.map_rel_iff\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Hypercubes and Mycielskians","header":"5.5.2. Embeddings and Maps of Mycielskians","id":"/Maps/#section-embeddings-and-maps-of-mycielskians"},"/Walks/#section-counting-bijections-and-a-crossing-pair":{"contents":"We now prove a general counting lemma to help with the informal argument we gave\nabove. If p is a path containing every neighbour of a vertex x, and f : ℕ → ℕ\nmaps {i | i < p.length} into {i | i ≤ p.length}, is injective on\n{n | n < p.length} and is surjective on {j | j ≤ p.length ∧ p.getVert j ≠ x},\nthen the indices i < p.length with G.Adj (p.getVert (f i)) x number exactly\nG.degree x. As we can see in its proof, the bijection is i ↦ p.getVert (f i) onto\nthe neighbours of x.\n\nopen Finset\n\nlemma Walk.IsPath.card_filter_adj_getVert_eq_degree [DecidableRel G.Adj] {u v x : V}\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet x)]\n    {p : G.Walk u v} (hp : p.IsPath) (hall : ∀ w, G.Adj x w → w ∈ p.support)\n    (f : ℕ → ℕ)\n    (hf_le : ∀ i ∈ range p.length, f i ≤ p.length)\n    (hf_inj : Set.InjOn f (range p.length))\n    (hf_surj : ∀ j ≤ p.length, p.getVert j ≠ x → ∃ i ∈ range p.length, f i = j) :\n    {i ∈ range p.length | G.Adj (p.getVert (f i)) x}.card = G.degree x := by\n  rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree]\n  refine card_nbij (fun i => p.getVert (f i)) (fun i hi => ?_)\n    (fun i₁ hi₁ i₂ hi₂ heq => ?_) ?_\n  · -- i ↦ p.getVert (f i) maps the indices of neighbours of x to\n    -- neighbours of x\n    simpa [adj_comm] using (mem_filter.1 hi).2\n  · -- it is injective on the indices of neighbours of x\n    rw [coe_filter, Set.mem_ofPred_eq] at hi₁ hi₂\n    exact hf_inj hi₁.1 hi₂.1 (hp.getVert_injOn (hf_le _ hi₁.1) (hf_le _ hi₂.1) heq)\n  · -- it is surjective onto the neighbours of x\n    intro w hw\n    rw [mem_coe, mem_neighborFinset] at hw\n    obtain ⟨n, rfl, hnle⟩ := mem_support_iff_exists_getVert.mp (hall _ hw)\n    obtain ⟨i, hi, rfl⟩ := hf_surj n hnle hw.ne'\n    exact ⟨i, mem_filter.2 ⟨hi, hw.symm⟩, rfl⟩\n\n\nThe indices i < p.length with G.Adj (p.getVert (i + 1)) u biject with the\nneighbours of u, hence number exactly G.degree u. (This is the previous lemma\nwith x = u and f i = i + 1.)\n\nlemma Walk.IsPath.card_succ_adj_start [DecidableRel G.Adj] {u v : V}\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet u)]\n    {p : G.Walk u v} (hp : p.IsPath) (hall : ∀ w, G.Adj u w → w ∈ p.support) :\n    {i ∈ range p.length | G.Adj (p.getVert (i + 1)) u}.card = G.degree u := by\n  refine hp.card_filter_adj_getVert_eq_degree hall (· + 1)\n    (fun i hi => by grind)\n    (fun a _ b _ h => by grind) ?_\n  intro n hn hne\n  have hn0 : n ≠ 0 := fun h0 => hne ((hp.getVert_eq_start_iff hn).mpr h0)\n  exact ⟨n - 1, by grind⟩\n\n\nThe indices i < p.length with G.Adj (p.getVert i) v biject with the neighbours\nof v, hence number exactly G.degree v. (This is\nIsPath.card_filter_adj_getVert_eq_degree with x = v and f i = i.)\n\nlemma Walk.IsPath.card_adj_end [DecidableRel G.Adj] {u v : V}\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)]\n    {p : G.Walk u v} (hp : p.IsPath) (hall : ∀ w, G.Adj v w → w ∈ p.support) :\n    {i ∈ range p.length | G.Adj (p.getVert i) v}.card = G.degree v := by\n  refine hp.card_filter_adj_getVert_eq_degree hall id\n    (fun i hi => (mem_range.mp hi).le) (fun a _ b _ h => h) ?_\n  intro n hn hne\n  have hnl : n ≠ p.length := fun hL => hne ((hp.getVert_eq_end_iff hn).mpr hL)\n  exact ⟨n, mem_range.mpr (by grind), rfl⟩\n\n\nThe next two results are about a maximal path. We define this as\nWalk.IsMaximalPath, a structure extending Walk.IsPath. (The end field is\nwritten «end» because end is a Lean keyword; French quotes turn it into an\nordinary name.)\n\n(Compare Walk.IsMaximumPath in the exercises: that is the stronger condition\nthat no path anywhere in G is longer.)\n\nA maximal path: a path that cannot be extended at either end, or equivalently\none for which every neighbour of either endpoint already lies on it./-- A *maximal path*: a path that cannot be extended at either end, or equivalently\none for which every neighbour of either endpoint already lies on it. -/\nstructure Walk.IsMaximalPath {u v : V} (p : G.Walk u v) : Prop extends\n  isPath : p.IsPath where\n  /-- Every neighbour of the start vertex lies on the path. -/\n  start : ∀ w, G.Adj u w → w ∈ p.support\n  /-- Every neighbour of the end vertex lies on the path. -/\n  «end» : ∀ w, G.Adj v w → w ∈ p.support\n\n\nFor a maximal path whose endpoints satisfy Ore's degree sum condition, pigeonhole on\nthe two counting lemmas gives the crossing pair: an index i with both\nG.Adj (p.getVert i) v and G.Adj (p.getVert (i + 1)) u.\n\nlemma Walk.IsMaximalPath.exists_crossing_index [Fintype V] {u v : V}\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet u)] [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)] {p : G.Walk u v}\n    (hmax : p.IsMaximalPath) (hcard : Fintype.card V ≤ G.degree u + G.degree v) :\n    ∃ i, G.Adj (p.getVert i) v ∧ G.Adj (p.getVert (i + 1)) u := by\n  classical\n  let A := {i ∈ range p.length | G.Adj (p.getVert (i + 1)) u}\n  let B := {i ∈ range p.length | G.Adj (p.getVert i) v}\n  have hA : A.card = G.degree u := hmax.isPath.card_succ_adj_start hmax.start\n  have hB : B.card = G.degree v := hmax.isPath.card_adj_end hmax.end\n  -- A path has ≤ card V distinct vertices, so p.length < card V.\n  have hlen : p.length + 1 ≤ Fintype.card V := hmax.isPath.length_lt\n  -- A and B both sit inside range p.length, but |A| + |B| ≥ card V > length.\n  have hsub : A ∪ B ⊆ range p.length :=\n    union_subset (filter_subset _ _) (filter_subset _ _)\n  have hcardU : (A ∪ B).card ≤ p.length :=\n    (card_le_card hsub).trans_eq (card_range _)\n  have hunion := card_union_add_card_inter A B\n  obtain ⟨i, hi⟩ : (A ∩ B).Nonempty := by rw [← card_pos]; grind\n  grind\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Hamiltonian Graphs: Ore, Dirac and Bondy–Chvátal*","header":"8.8.2. Counting Bijections and a Crossing Pair","id":"/Walks/#section-counting-bijections-and-a-crossing-pair"},"/Walks/#section-exercises-walks":{"contents":"1. Prove by induction on p that reversing a walk does not change its length.\nHint: reverse_cons and length_append.\n\nend SimpleGraph\nend\nnamespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\nopen HypercubeVert Walk\n\nexample {u v : V} (p : G.Walk u v) : p.reverse.length = p.length := by\n  sorry\n\n\n2. Prove by induction on p that the support of p.append q is the support of p\nfollowed by the tail of the support of q (the tail drops the shared vertex v).\nHint: in the nil case, cons_tail_support.\n\nexample {u v w : V} (p : G.Walk u v) (q : G.Walk v w) :\n    (p.append q).support = p.support ++ q.support.tail := by\n  sorry\n\n\n3. Show that the nil walk is a trail.\n\nexample (u : V) : (nil : G.Walk u u).IsTrail := by\n  sorry\n\n\n4. Show that if p : G.Walk u v has length 0 then u = v. Hint: use cases p.\n\nexample {u v : V} (p : G.Walk u v) (hl : p.length = 0) : u = v := by\n  sorry\n\n\n5. Show that splitting a walk at a vertex on its support preserves the total length.\nHint: Walk.take_spec.\n\nexample [DecidableEq V] {u v w : V} (p : G.Walk u v) (h : w ∈ p.support) :\n    (p.takeUntil w h).length + (p.dropUntil w h).length = p.length := by\n  sorry\n\n\n6. The walk eulerC5 travels once around the cycle C[5]. Show that it is an\nEulerian circuit. Hint: decide proves that it is Eulerian, using the Decidable\ninstance from the chapter. For IsCircuit, rewrite with isCircuit_def and\nisTrail_def first.\n\nThe closed walk 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 0 in the cycle C[5]./-- The closed walk 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 0 in the cycle C[5]. -/\nprivate def eulerC5 : C[5].Walk 0 0 :=\n  cons (show C[5].Adj 0 1 by decide) <| cons (show C[5].Adj 1 2 by decide) <|\n    cons (show C[5].Adj 2 3 by decide) <| cons (show C[5].Adj 3 4 by decide) <|\n      cons (by decide) nil\n\n\nexample : eulerC5.IsEulerian ∧ eulerC5.IsCircuit := by\n  sorry\n\n\n7. Show that the tail of a cycle is never nil. Hint: a cycle has length at least\nthree.\n\nlemma Walk.IsCycle.tail_not_nil' {u : V} {c : G.Walk u u} (hc : c.IsCycle) :\n    ¬ c.tail.Nil := by\n  sorry\n\n\n8. A cons walk is a Hamiltonian cycle iff its tail is Hamiltonian and has length at\nleast two.\n\nlemma Walk.cons_isHamiltonianCycle_iff' [DecidableEq V] {u v : V} (p : G.Walk v u)\n    (h : G.Adj u v) :\n    (cons h p).IsHamiltonianCycle ↔ p.IsHamiltonian ∧ 2 ≤ p.length := by\n  sorry\n\n\n9. Show that reversing a copy of a walk is the same as copying its reverse. Hint:\nsubst_vars.\n\nexample {u v u' v' : V} (p : G.Walk u v) (hu : u = u') (hv : v = v') :\n    (p.copy hu hv).reverse = p.reverse.copy hv hu := by\n  sorry\n\n\n10. Show that a walk is not nil iff it has positive length. Hint: use cases p;\nthen Nil.nil and nofun are useful.\n\nexample {u v : V} (p : G.Walk u v) : ¬ p.Nil ↔ 0 < p.length := by\n  sorry\n\n\n11. Show that an injective homomorphism maps trails to trails. Mathlib has this as\nIsTrail.map, but prove it directly. Hint: isTrail_def, edges_map and\nList.Nodup.map.\n\nexample {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {f : G →g H} (hf : Function.Injective f)\n    {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v} (hp : p.IsTrail) : (p.map f).IsTrail := by\n  sorry\n\n\n12. Translating the walk pathNat.path 0 j by i, with Walk.map along\npathNat.translate i, gives a walk from 0 + i to (0 + j) + i, so before we can\ncompare it with pathNat.path i j we must copy it. Show that the two walks are\nthen equal. Hint: compare supports with Walk.ext_support, then use support_copy,\nWalk.support_map and pathNat.support_path.\n\nexample (i j : ℕ) :\n    ((pathNat.path 0 j).map (pathNat.translate i).toHom).copy\n      (by simp [pathNat.translate]) (by simp [pathNat.translate, add_comm]) =\n      pathNat.path i j := by\n  sorry\n\n\n13. If p is a Hamiltonian walk of length at least two and there is an edge h₁\nfrom its i-th vertex to its end and an edge h₂ from its (i + 1)-th vertex to\nits start, then p.posaClosed h₁ h₂ is a Hamiltonian cycle.\n\nlemma Walk.IsHamiltonian.isHamiltonianCycle_posaClosed' {u v} {i} [DecidableEq V]\n    {p : G.Walk u v} (hp : p.IsHamiltonian) (h2 : 2 ≤ p.length)\n    (h₁ : G.Adj (p.getVert i) v) (h₂ : G.Adj (p.getVert (i + 1)) u) :\n    (p.posaClosed h₁ h₂).IsHamiltonianCycle := by\n  sorry\n\n\n14. Show that the Hamming distance on the hypercube is symmetric.\n\nlemma HypercubeVert.hammingDist_comm' {n : ℕ} (u v : HypercubeVert n) :\n    u.hammingDist v = v.hammingDist u := by\n  sorry\n\n\nThe next few exercises are about the Gray code walk hypercube.grayPath and showing\nhow to build a Hamiltonian cycle in Q[n + 2] from it.\n\nThe Gray code Hamiltonian cycle in Q[n + 2]./-- The Gray code Hamiltonian cycle in Q[n + 2]. -/\ndef hypercube.grayCycle (n : ℕ) :\n    Q[n + 2].Walk (grayStart (n + 2)) (grayStart (n + 2)) :=\n  have adj : Q[n + 2].Adj (grayStart (n + 2)) (grayEnd (n + 2)):= by simp\n  Walk.cons adj (grayPath (n + 2)).reverse\n\n\n15. Show that in the Gray code walk hypercube.grayPath (n + 2), the second vertex\nis always in the false slice of Q[n + 2].\n\nlemma hypercube.grayPath_snd_false' (n : ℕ) : (grayPath (n + 2)).snd.2 = false := by\n  sorry\n\n\n16. Show that hypercube.grayCycle n is indeed a cycle.\n\nlemma hypercube.isCycle_grayCycle' (n : ℕ) : (grayCycle n).IsCycle := by\n  sorry\n\n\n17. Show that hypercube.grayCycle n is a Hamiltonian cycle.\n\nlemma hypercube.isHamiltonianCycle_grayCycle' (n : ℕ) :\n    (grayCycle n).IsHamiltonianCycle := by\n  sorry\n\n\n18. Prove that Q[n] is Hamiltonian unless n = 1. (Recall that Q[0] is a single\nvertex which is considered Hamiltonian.)\n\nlemma isHamiltonian_hypercube' {n : ℕ} (h1 : n ≠ 1) : Q[n].IsHamiltonian :=\n  sorry\n\n\n19. Show that the Grötzsch graph mμ[C[5]] is Hamiltonian. Hint:\nIsHamiltonian.mycielskian.\n\nexample : grotzschGraph.IsHamiltonian := by\n  sorry\n\n\nThe next three exercises return to the example from the chapter's closure section:\nC[5] ⊔ edge 0 2 ⊔ edge 0 3, i.e. C[5] with the two chords 0 — 2 and 0 — 3.\nMathlib's Decidable instance for adjacency in edge u v is currently defined using\na cast, so decide gets stuck on any graph built from edge (see\nDecidability and Kernel Reduction). So we redefine the graph using\nfromRel instead.\n\nC[5] with the two chords 0 — 2 and 0 — 3./-- C[5] with the two chords 0 — 2 and 0 — 3. -/\nabbrev chordedC5 : SimpleGraph (Fin 5) :=\n  C[5] ⊔ fromRel fun x y => x = 0 ∧ (y = 2 ∨ y = 3)\n\n\n20. Check that chordedC5 is the graph from the chapter. Hint: after ext, simplify\nthe adjacency relation and then decide +revert should complete the proof.\n\nexample : chordedC5 = C[5] ⊔ edge 0 2 ⊔ edge 0 3 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n21. Show that chordedC5 is not an Ore graph.\n\nexample : ¬ chordedC5.IsOreGraph := by\n  sorry\n\n\n22. Show that the closure of chordedC5 is complete, even though chordedC5 is not\nan Ore graph. Hint: ext and decide +revert.\n\nexample : chordedC5.closure = ⊤ := by\n  sorry\n\n\nThe remaining exercises develop the classical direct proof of Ore's theorem via\nmaximum-length paths. The chapter's crossing-pair machinery\n(Walk.IsMaximalPath.exists_cycle_spanning) will be useful here; the exercises\nsupply the extremal argument that replaces the closure. We begin with some\nwarm-up properties of Ore graphs.\n\n23. In an Ore graph any two distinct vertices are either adjacent or have a common\nneighbour. Hint: if distinct vertices u and v are non-adjacent and have no\ncommon neighbour, then their neighbourhoods are disjoint subsets of V \\ {u, v},\nso their degree sum is at most |V| - 2, contradicting the Ore condition.\n\nopen Finset in\nlemma IsOreGraph.adj_or_exists_adj_adj' [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (h : G.IsOreGraph) {u v : V} (hne : u ≠ v) :\n    G.Adj u v ∨ ∃ w, G.Adj u w ∧ G.Adj v w := by\n  sorry\n\n\n24. Consequently any two vertices of an Ore graph are joined by a walk of length\nat most two (in the language of later chapters: an Ore graph is connected, with\ndiameter at most two).\n\nlemma IsOreGraph.exists_walk_length_le_two' [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (h : G.IsOreGraph) (u v : V) : ∃ p : G.Walk u v, p.length ≤ 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n25. An Ore graph on at least three vertices contains a vertex of degree at least\ntwo. Hint: among any three distinct vertices either some pair is non-adjacent, and\nso the Ore condition forces its degree sum to be at least three, or all three are\npairwise adjacent.\n\nlemma IsOreGraph.exists_two_le_degree' [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (h : G.IsOreGraph) (hn : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V) : ∃ w, 2 ≤ G.degree w := by\n  sorry\n\n\n26. Existence of a vertex of degree at least two implies existence of a path of\nlength at least two.\n\nlemma Walk.exists_isPath_of_two_le_degree' [G.LocallyFinite] {v : V}\n    (hv : 2 ≤ G.degree v) :\n    ∃ (x y : V) (q : G.Walk x y), q.IsPath ∧ 2 ≤ q.length := by\n  sorry\n\n\nMathlib proves the existence of a maximum-length path in a graph with\nnonempty vertex set and finite edge set:\nWalk.exists_isPath_forall_isPath_length_le_length. Notice that the signature of\nthis lemma doesn't actually mention the word \"maximum\" but spells it out\nexplicitly. Since we will use this idea repeatedly we make a definition, bundling\ntogether the fact that p is a path with the maximality of its length. (A note\non terminology: such a p has maximum length: no path anywhere in G is\nlonger. This is stronger than being maximal, which would only say that p\nitself cannot be extended to a longer path.)\n\nA maximum-length path: a path such that no path in G is longer./-- A *maximum-length path*: a path such that no path in G is longer. -/\nstructure Walk.IsMaximumPath {u v : V} (p : G.Walk u v) : Prop extends\n  isPath : p.IsPath where\n  length_le : ∀ ⦃x y : V⦄ ⦃q : G.Walk x y⦄, q.IsPath → q.length ≤ p.length\n\n\n27. Restate Mathlib's existence result compactly.\n\nvariable (G) in\nlemma Walk.exists_isMaximumPath' [Nonempty V] [Finite G.edgeSet] :\n    ∃ (u v : V) (p : G.Walk u v), p.IsMaximumPath := by\n  sorry\n\n\n28. The reverse of a maximum-length path is again a maximum-length path.\n\nlemma Walk.IsMaximumPath.reverse' {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v} (h : p.IsMaximumPath) :\n    p.reverse.IsMaximumPath := by\n  sorry\n\n\n29. A maximum-length path in an Ore graph on at least three vertices has length\nat least two.\n\nlemma IsOreGraph.two_le_length_of_isMaximumPath' [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    {u v} (h : G.IsOreGraph)\n    (hn : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V) {p : G.Walk u v} (hmax : p.IsMaximumPath) :\n    2 ≤ p.length := by\n  sorry\n\n\n30. A maximum-length path is in particular a maximal path, in the sense of the\nchapter's Walk.IsMaximalPath: neither endpoint has a neighbour missing from it.\nHint: if a neighbour of the start were missing then cons would give a strictly\nlonger path; for the end vertex apply the same argument to p.reverse, using\nIsMaximumPath.reverse.\n\nlemma Walk.IsMaximumPath.isMaximalPath' {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v}\n    (hmax : p.IsMaximumPath) : p.IsMaximalPath := by\n  sorry\n\n\n31. If p is a walk from a vertex in a set S to a vertex outside of S, there\nare adjacent vertices c ∈ S and d ∉ S. Hint: Walk.exists_boundary_dart.\n\nlemma Walk.exists_cross_adj' {a b} {S : Set V} (p : G.Walk a b) (ha : a ∈ S)\n    (hb : b ∉ S) : ∃ c ∈ S, ∃ d ∉ S, G.Adj c d  := by\n  sorry\n\n\n32. In a non-nil closed walk the first vertex also occurs as the last, so a vertex\nlies in the tail of the support iff it lies in the support.\n\nlemma Walk.mem_support_tail_iff' {a : V} {c : G.Walk a a} (hc : ¬c.Nil) {x : V} :\n    x ∈ c.support.tail ↔ x ∈ c.support := by\n  sorry\n\n\n33. A cycle can be cut at any of its vertices to give a path from that vertex\nvisiting exactly the cycle's vertices, of length one less than the cycle. Hint:\nrotate the cycle to start at y and take the reversed tail, as in the proof of the\nchapter's IsCycle.exists_isPath_of_mem_edges.\n\nlemma Walk.IsCycle.exists_isPath_spanning' {a : V} {c : G.Walk a a}\n    (hc : c.IsCycle) {y : V} (hy : y ∈ c.support) :\n    ∃ (z : V) (q : G.Walk y z), q.IsPath ∧ q.length + 1 = c.length ∧\n      (∀ x, x ∈ q.support ↔ x ∈ c.support) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n34. If p is a maximum-length path from u, every vertex is reachable from u,\nand some cycle through u of length one more than p visits exactly the vertices\nof p, then p is Hamiltonian. (Neither Ore's condition nor finiteness is needed.)\nHint: if p missed a vertex, a walk from u to it and exists_cross_adj would\nyield an edge leaving the cycle. Break the cycle with exists_isPath_spanning and\nextend the resulting path with this edge to give a strictly longer path,\ncontradicting maximality.\n\nlemma Walk.IsMaximumPath.isHamiltonian_of_isCycle' [DecidableEq V] {u v : V}\n    {p : G.Walk u v} (hmax : p.IsMaximumPath) (hreach : ∀ w, G.Reachable u w)\n    {c : G.Walk u u} (hc : c.IsCycle) (hlen : c.length = p.length + 1)\n    (hsupp : ∀ x, x ∈ c.support ↔ x ∈ p.support) : p.IsHamiltonian := by\n  sorry\n\n\n35. A maximum-length path in an Ore graph is Hamiltonian. Hint: apply\nisHamiltonian_of_isCycle. Ore's condition supplies the cycle, via\ntwo_le_length_of_isMaximumPath and IsMaximalPath.exists_cycle_spanning (from the\nchapter), and the reachability, via exists_walk_length_le_two.\n\nlemma Walk.isHamiltonian_of_isMaximumPath' [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    [G.LocallyFinite] (h : G.IsOreGraph)\n    (hn : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V) {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v}\n    (hmax : p.IsMaximumPath) : p.IsHamiltonian := by\n  sorry\n\n\n36. Give a direct proof of Ore's theorem, not using the closure: take a\nmaximum-length path; it is Hamiltonian of length ≥ 2, so\nIsHamiltonian.exists_isHamiltonianCycle_of_isOrePair closes it into a Hamiltonian\ncycle.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [G.LocallyFinite] (h : G.IsOreGraph)\n    (hn : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V) : G.IsHamiltonian := by\n  sorry\n\n\n1. Prove by induction on p that reversing a walk does not change its length.\nHint: reverse_cons and length_append.example {u v : V} (p : G.Walk u v) : p.reverse.length = p.length := by\n  induction p with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons h p ih =>\n    rw [reverse_cons, length_append, ih, length_cons, length_cons, length_nil]\n2. Prove by induction on p that the support of p.append q is the support of p\nfollowed by the tail of the support of q (the tail drops the shared vertex v).\nHint: in the nil case, cons_tail_support.example {u v w : V} (p : G.Walk u v) (q : G.Walk v w) :\n    (p.append q).support = p.support ++ q.support.tail := by\n  induction p with\n  | nil => rw [nil_append, support_nil, List.singleton_append, cons_tail_support]\n  | cons h p ih => rw [cons_append, support_cons, support_cons, ih, List.cons_append]\n3. Show that the nil walk is a trail.example (u : V) : (nil : G.Walk u u).IsTrail := .nil\n4. Show that if p : G.Walk u v has length 0 then u = v. Hint: use cases p.example {u v : V} (p : G.Walk u v) (hl : p.length = 0) : u = v := by\n  cases p with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons h p => simp at hl\n5. Show that splitting a walk at a vertex on its support preserves the total length.\nHint: Walk.take_spec.example [DecidableEq V] {u v w : V} (p : G.Walk u v) (h : w ∈ p.support) :\n    (p.takeUntil w h).length + (p.dropUntil w h).length = p.length := by\n  rw [← length_append, take_spec]\n6. The walk eulerC5 travels once around the cycle C[5]. Show that it is an\nEulerian circuit. Hint: decide proves that it is Eulerian, using the Decidable\ninstance from the chapter. For IsCircuit, rewrite with isCircuit_def and\nisTrail_def first.example : eulerC5.IsEulerian ∧ eulerC5.IsCircuit := by\n  rw [isCircuit_def, isTrail_def]\n  decide\n7. Show that the tail of a cycle is never nil. Hint: a cycle has length at least\nthree.lemma Walk.IsCycle.tail_not_nil {u : V} {c : G.Walk u u} (hc : c.IsCycle) :\n    ¬ c.tail.Nil := by\n  grind [length_tail_add_one, hc.three_le_length]\n8. A cons walk is a Hamiltonian cycle iff its tail is Hamiltonian and has length at\nleast two.lemma Walk.cons_isHamiltonianCycle_iff [DecidableEq V] {u v : V} (p : G.Walk v u)\n    (h : G.Adj u v) :\n    (cons h p).IsHamiltonianCycle ↔ p.IsHamiltonian ∧ 2 ≤ p.length := by\n  rw [isHamiltonianCycle_isCycle_and_isHamiltonian_tail,\n    cons_isCycle_iff_length_ne_one, and_assoc]\n  constructor <;> rintro ⟨h1, h2⟩\n  · exact ⟨by simpa using h2.2, by grind⟩\n  · exact ⟨h1.isPath, by grind, by simpa using h1⟩\n9. Show that reversing a copy of a walk is the same as copying its reverse. Hint:\nsubst_vars.example {u v u' v' : V} (p : G.Walk u v) (hu : u = u') (hv : v = v') :\n    (p.copy hu hv).reverse = p.reverse.copy hv hu := by\n  subst_vars\n  rfl\n10. Show that a walk is not nil iff it has positive length. Hint: use cases p;\nthen Nil.nil and nofun are useful.example {u v : V} (p : G.Walk u v) : ¬ p.Nil ↔ 0 < p.length := by\n  cases p with\n  | nil => exact ⟨fun h => (h Nil.nil).elim, fun h => (Nat.lt_irrefl 0 h).elim⟩\n  | cons h p => exact ⟨fun _ => by simp, fun _ => nofun⟩\n11. Show that an injective homomorphism maps trails to trails. Mathlib has this as\nIsTrail.map, but prove it directly. Hint: isTrail_def, edges_map and\nList.Nodup.map.example {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {f : G →g H} (hf : Function.Injective f)\n    {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v} (hp : p.IsTrail) : (p.map f).IsTrail := by\n  rw [isTrail_def, edges_map]\n  exact hp.edges_nodup.map (Sym2.map.injective hf)\n12. Translating the walk pathNat.path 0 j by i, with Walk.map along\npathNat.translate i, gives a walk from 0 + i to (0 + j) + i, so before we can\ncompare it with pathNat.path i j we must copy it. Show that the two walks are\nthen equal. Hint: compare supports with Walk.ext_support, then use support_copy,\nWalk.support_map and pathNat.support_path.example (i j : ℕ) :\n    ((pathNat.path 0 j).map (pathNat.translate i).toHom).copy\n      (by simp [pathNat.translate]) (by simp [pathNat.translate, add_comm]) =\n      pathNat.path i j := by\n  apply Walk.ext_support\n  rw [support_copy, Walk.support_map, pathNat.support_path, pathNat.support_path]\n  have h : ⇑(pathNat.translate i).toHom = (i + ·) := funext fun k => add_comm k i\n  rw [h, List.map_add_range', add_zero]\n13. If p is a Hamiltonian walk of length at least two and there is an edge h₁\nfrom its i-th vertex to its end and an edge h₂ from its (i + 1)-th vertex to\nits start, then p.posaClosed h₁ h₂ is a Hamiltonian cycle.lemma Walk.IsHamiltonian.isHamiltonianCycle_posaClosed {u v} {i} [DecidableEq V]\n    {p : G.Walk u v} (hp : p.IsHamiltonian) (h2 : 2 ≤ p.length)\n    (h₁ : G.Adj (p.getVert i) v) (h₂ : G.Adj (p.getVert (i + 1)) u) :\n    (p.posaClosed h₁ h₂).IsHamiltonianCycle := by\n  have : Fintype V := hp.fintype\n  apply isHamiltonianCycle_iff_isCycle_and_length_eq.2\n          ⟨hp.isPath.isCycle_posaClosed h2 h₁ h₂, ?_⟩\n  simpa using hp.length_support\n14. Show that the Hamming distance on the hypercube is symmetric.lemma HypercubeVert.hammingDist_comm {n : ℕ} (u v : HypercubeVert n) :\n    u.hammingDist v = v.hammingDist u := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => rfl\n  | succ n ih =>\n    rcases u with ⟨u, b⟩\n    rcases v with ⟨v, c⟩\n    grind [HypercubeVert.hammingDist]\n15. Show that in the Gray code walk hypercube.grayPath (n + 2), the second vertex\nis always in the false slice of Q[n + 2].lemma hypercube.grayPath_snd_false (n : ℕ) : (grayPath (n + 2)).snd.2 = false := by\n  -- the first half of grayPath (n + 2), exactly as in its definition\n  let fH : Q[n + 2].Walk (grayStart (n + 2)) (grayEnd (n + 1), false) :=\n    ((grayPath (n + 1)).map (hypercube.embedFalse (n + 1)).toHom).copy\n      (by simp) (by simp)\n  have : fH.length = 2 ^ (n + 1) - 1 := by\n    rw [length_copy, length_map, length_grayPath]\n  have hstep : (grayPath (n + 2)).snd = (fH.append _).getVert 1 := rfl\n  cases n with\n  | zero => rfl\n  | succ n =>\n    have : 1 < fH.length := by grind\n    rw [hstep, Walk.getVert_append, ite_eq_left this]\n    simp [fH, getVert_map]\n16. Show that hypercube.grayCycle n is indeed a cycle.@[simp]\nlemma hypercube.isCycle_grayCycle (n : ℕ) : (grayCycle n).IsCycle := by\n  apply (cons_isCycle_iff _ _).2 ⟨(isPath_grayPath (n + 2)).reverse, ?_⟩\n  rw [edges_reverse, List.mem_reverse]\n  intro hmem\n  have hf := grayPath_snd_false n\n  rw [← (isPath_grayPath (n + 2)).eq_snd_of_mem_edges hmem] at hf\n  contradiction\n17. Show that hypercube.grayCycle n is a Hamiltonian cycle.lemma hypercube.isHamiltonianCycle_grayCycle (n : ℕ) :\n    (grayCycle n).IsHamiltonianCycle := by\n  apply (cons_isHamiltonianCycle_iff _ _).2 ⟨?_, ?_⟩\n  · exact isHamiltonian_reverse.2 <| isHamiltonian_grayPath (n + 2)\n  · grind [length_grayPath, length_reverse]\n18. Prove that Q[n] is Hamiltonian unless n = 1. (Recall that Q[0] is a single\nvertex which is considered Hamiltonian.)lemma isHamiltonian_hypercube {n : ℕ} (h1 : n ≠ 1) : Q[n].IsHamiltonian :=\n  match n with\n  | 0 => fun h => by simp at h\n  | 1 => (h1 rfl).elim\n  | n + 2 => fun _ => ⟨_, _, hypercube.isHamiltonianCycle_grayCycle n⟩\n19. Show that the Grötzsch graph mμ[C[5]] is Hamiltonian. Hint:\nIsHamiltonian.mycielskian.example : grotzschGraph.IsHamiltonian :=\n  IsHamiltonian.mycielskian (isHamiltonian_cycleGraph (by grind)) (by simp)\n20. Check that chordedC5 is the graph from the chapter. Hint: after ext, simplify\nthe adjacency relation and then decide +revert should complete the proof.example : chordedC5 = C[5] ⊔ edge 0 2 ⊔ edge 0 3 := by\n  ext x y\n  simp only [sup_adj, fromRel_adj, edge_adj]\n  decide +revert\n21. Show that chordedC5 is not an Ore graph.example : ¬ chordedC5.IsOreGraph := by\n  -- the non-adjacent vertices 1 and 4 have degree sum 4 < 5\n  intro h\n  have h14 := h 1 4\n  -- contrapose! h14 swaps the hypothesis h14 and the goal False, negating both\n  -- (plain contrapose does the same without simplifying the negations)\n  contrapose! h14\n  unfold IsOrePair\n  decide\n22. Show that the closure of chordedC5 is complete, even though chordedC5 is not\nan Ore graph. Hint: ext and decide +revert.example : chordedC5.closure = ⊤ := by\n  ext x y\n  simp only [top_adj]\n  decide +revert\n23. In an Ore graph any two distinct vertices are either adjacent or have a common\nneighbour. Hint: if distinct vertices u and v are non-adjacent and have no\ncommon neighbour, then their neighbourhoods are disjoint subsets of V \\ {u, v},\nso their degree sum is at most |V| - 2, contradicting the Ore condition.open Finset in\nlemma IsOreGraph.adj_or_exists_adj_adj [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (h : G.IsOreGraph) {u v : V} (hne : u ≠ v) :\n    G.Adj u v ∨ ∃ w, G.Adj u w ∧ G.Adj v w := by\n  classical\n  by_cases huv : G.Adj u v\n  · exact Or.inl huv\n  refine Or.inr ?_\n  -- The union of the neighbourhoods of u and v lives in V \\ {u, v}\n  have h_sub : G.neighborFinset u ∪ G.neighborFinset v ⊆ univ \\ {u, v} := by\n    intro x hx\n    simp only [mem_union, mem_neighborFinset, mem_sdiff, mem_univ, true_and,\n      mem_insert, mem_singleton] at hx ⊢\n    grind [adj_symm]\n  -- So the cardinality of the union of the neighbourhoods is at most |V| - 2\n  have h_card := card_le_card h_sub\n  -- The cardinality of V \\ {u, v} is exactly |V| - 2\n  have h_univ_card : (univ \\ {u, v} : Finset V).card = Fintype.card V - 2 := by\n    grind\n  -- there is w in the intersection of the neighbourhoods of u and v\n  obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ : (G.neighborFinset u ∩ G.neighborFinset v).Nonempty := by\n    by_contra! h_empty\n    rw [← disjoint_iff_inter_eq_empty] at h_empty\n    -- Evaluate the cardinality of the subset relation\n    rw [card_union_of_disjoint h_empty] at h_card\n    -- Since G.degree x is the cardinality of G.neighborFinset x,\n    -- grind can close the arithmetic contradiction directly using Ore's\n    -- condition at the pair u, v\n    grind [card_neighborFinset_eq_degree, h u v hne huv]\n  exact ⟨w, by simpa using hw⟩\n24. Consequently any two vertices of an Ore graph are joined by a walk of length\nat most two (in the language of later chapters: an Ore graph is connected, with\ndiameter at most two).lemma IsOreGraph.exists_walk_length_le_two [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (h : G.IsOreGraph) (u v : V) : ∃ p : G.Walk u v, p.length ≤ 2 := by\n  by_cases heq : u = v\n  · exact heq ▸ ⟨nil, by simp⟩\n  · rcases h.adj_or_exists_adj_adj heq with  huv | ⟨w, h1, h2⟩\n    · exact ⟨cons huv nil, by simp⟩\n    · exact ⟨cons h1 (cons h2.symm nil), by simp⟩\n25. An Ore graph on at least three vertices contains a vertex of degree at least\ntwo. Hint: among any three distinct vertices either some pair is non-adjacent, and\nso the Ore condition forces its degree sum to be at least three, or all three are\npairwise adjacent.open Finset in\nlemma IsOreGraph.exists_two_le_degree [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (h : G.IsOreGraph) (hn : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V) : ∃ w, 2 ≤ G.degree w := by\n  obtain ⟨a, b, c, hab, hac, hbc⟩ := Fintype.two_lt_card_iff.mp hn\n  by_cases hadj : G.Adj a b\n  · by_cases hadj' : G.Adj b c\n    · refine ⟨b, ?_⟩\n      rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree]\n      exact one_lt_card_iff.2 ⟨a, c, by simp [hadj.symm, hadj', hac]⟩\n    · have : 3 ≤ G.degree b + G.degree c := hn.trans (h b c hbc hadj')\n      grind\n  · have : 3 ≤ G.degree a + G.degree b := hn.trans (h a b hab hadj)\n    grind\n26. Existence of a vertex of degree at least two implies existence of a path of\nlength at least two.lemma Walk.exists_isPath_of_two_le_degree [G.LocallyFinite] {v : V}\n    (hv : 2 ≤ G.degree v) :\n    ∃ (x y : V) (q : G.Walk x y), q.IsPath ∧ 2 ≤ q.length := by\n  rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree] at hv\n  obtain ⟨x, y, hx, hy, hxy⟩ := Finset.one_lt_card_iff.1 hv\n  rw [mem_neighborFinset] at hx hy\n  exact ⟨x, y, cons hx.symm (cons hy nil), by simp_all [hx.symm.ne, hy.ne]⟩\n27. Restate Mathlib's existence result compactly.variable (G) in\nlemma Walk.exists_isMaximumPath [Nonempty V] [Finite G.edgeSet] :\n    ∃ (u v : V) (p : G.Walk u v), p.IsMaximumPath :=\n  let ⟨u, v, p, hp, hmax⟩ := exists_isPath_forall_isPath_length_le_length G\n  ⟨u, v, p, hp, hmax⟩\n28. The reverse of a maximum-length path is again a maximum-length path.lemma Walk.IsMaximumPath.reverse {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v} (h : p.IsMaximumPath) :\n    p.reverse.IsMaximumPath :=\n  ⟨h.isPath.reverse, fun _ _ _ hq => p.length_reverse ▸ h.length_le hq⟩\n29. A maximum-length path in an Ore graph on at least three vertices has length\nat least two.lemma IsOreGraph.two_le_length_of_isMaximumPath [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    {u v} (h : G.IsOreGraph)\n    (hn : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V) {p : G.Walk u v} (hmax : p.IsMaximumPath) :\n    2 ≤ p.length := by\n  obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ := h.exists_two_le_degree hn\n  obtain ⟨x, y, q, hq, hlen⟩ := exists_isPath_of_two_le_degree hw\n  exact hlen.trans (hmax.length_le hq)\n30. A maximum-length path is in particular a maximal path, in the sense of the\nchapter's Walk.IsMaximalPath: neither endpoint has a neighbour missing from it.\nHint: if a neighbour of the start were missing then cons would give a strictly\nlonger path; for the end vertex apply the same argument to p.reverse, using\nIsMaximumPath.reverse.lemma Walk.IsMaximumPath.isMaximalPath {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v}\n    (hmax : p.IsMaximumPath) : p.IsMaximalPath := by\n  -- the start case, stated for an arbitrary maximum-length path so that we can\n  -- reuse it for the reverse of p\n  have hstart : ∀ {x y : V} {q : G.Walk x y}, q.IsMaximumPath →\n      ∀ w, G.Adj x w → w ∈ q.support := by\n    intro x y q hq w h\n    by_contra hw\n    simpa using hq.length_le <| (q.cons_isPath_iff h.symm).mpr ⟨hq.isPath, hw⟩\n  exact ⟨hmax.isPath, hstart hmax,\n    fun w h => by grind [hstart hmax.reverse w h, support_reverse]⟩\n31. If p is a walk from a vertex in a set S to a vertex outside of S, there\nare adjacent vertices c ∈ S and d ∉ S. Hint: Walk.exists_boundary_dart.lemma Walk.exists_cross_adj {a b} {S : Set V} (p : G.Walk a b) (ha : a ∈ S)\n    (hb : b ∉ S) : ∃ c ∈ S, ∃ d ∉ S, G.Adj c d  := by\n  obtain ⟨⟨⟨c, d⟩, hcd⟩, -, ⟨hc, hd⟩⟩ := p.exists_boundary_dart S ha hb\n  use c, hc, d, hd, hcd\n32. In a non-nil closed walk the first vertex also occurs as the last, so a vertex\nlies in the tail of the support iff it lies in the support.lemma Walk.mem_support_tail_iff {a : V} {c : G.Walk a a} (hc : ¬c.Nil) {x : V} :\n    x ∈ c.support.tail ↔ x ∈ c.support := by\n  refine ⟨List.mem_of_mem_tail, fun hx => ?_⟩\n  rw [← c.cons_tail_support] at hx\n  obtain rfl | h := List.mem_cons.mp hx\n  · exact c.end_mem_tail_support hc\n  · exact h\n33. A cycle can be cut at any of its vertices to give a path from that vertex\nvisiting exactly the cycle's vertices, of length one less than the cycle. Hint:\nrotate the cycle to start at y and take the reversed tail, as in the proof of the\nchapter's IsCycle.exists_isPath_of_mem_edges.lemma Walk.IsCycle.exists_isPath_spanning {a : V} {c : G.Walk a a}\n    (hc : c.IsCycle) {y : V} (hy : y ∈ c.support) :\n    ∃ (z : V) (q : G.Walk y z), q.IsPath ∧ q.length + 1 = c.length ∧\n      (∀ x, x ∈ q.support ↔ x ∈ c.support) := by\n  classical\n  -- Rotate the cycle to start at y.\n  have hd : (c.rotate y hy).IsCycle := (isCycle_rotate hy).mpr hc\n  -- The rotated cycle is not nil, and has the same length as the original cycle\n  have hdnn : ¬ (c.rotate y hy).Nil := hd.not_nil\n  have hlen : (c.rotate y hy).length = c.length := c.length_rotate _ hy\n  -- The path we want is the reversed tail of the rotated cycle, which has\n  -- the correct length and contains the same vertices as the original cycle.\n  refine ⟨_, (c.rotate y hy).tail.reverse, hd.isPath_tail.reverse, ?_, fun x => ?_⟩\n  · grind [length_tail_add_one, length_reverse]\n  · grind [support_reverse, support_tail_of_not_nil _ hdnn, ← mem_support_rotate_iff,\n            mem_support_tail_iff]\n34. If p is a maximum-length path from u, every vertex is reachable from u,\nand some cycle through u of length one more than p visits exactly the vertices\nof p, then p is Hamiltonian. (Neither Ore's condition nor finiteness is needed.)\nHint: if p missed a vertex, a walk from u to it and exists_cross_adj would\nyield an edge leaving the cycle. Break the cycle with exists_isPath_spanning and\nextend the resulting path with this edge to give a strictly longer path,\ncontradicting maximality.lemma Walk.IsMaximumPath.isHamiltonian_of_isCycle [DecidableEq V] {u v : V}\n    {p : G.Walk u v} (hmax : p.IsMaximumPath) (hreach : ∀ w, G.Reachable u w)\n    {c : G.Walk u u} (hc : c.IsCycle) (hlen : c.length = p.length + 1)\n    (hsupp : ∀ x, x ∈ c.support ↔ x ∈ p.support) : p.IsHamiltonian := by\n  rw [hmax.isPath.isHamiltonian_iff]\n  by_contra! hcon\n  -- Some vertex w is missing from the path.\n  obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ := hcon\n  -- The support of c (as a Set V) does not contain w\n  have hw' : w ∉ {x | x ∈ c.support} := fun hwc => hw ((hsupp w).mp hwc)\n  -- A boundary edge leaves c; break the cycle there and prepend it.\n  obtain ⟨q⟩ := hreach w\n  obtain ⟨x, hxc, z, hzc, hxz⟩ := q.exists_cross_adj c.start_mem_support hw'\n  obtain ⟨z', q, hqpath, hqlen, hqsupp⟩ := hc.exists_isPath_spanning hxc\n  have hznotin : z ∉ q.support := fun hzq => hzc ((hqsupp z).mp hzq)\n  have hlong : (q.cons hxz.symm).IsPath :=\n    (cons_isPath_iff hxz.symm q).mpr ⟨hqpath, hznotin⟩\n  grind [length_cons, hmax.length_le hlong]\n35. A maximum-length path in an Ore graph is Hamiltonian. Hint: apply\nisHamiltonian_of_isCycle. Ore's condition supplies the cycle, via\ntwo_le_length_of_isMaximumPath and IsMaximalPath.exists_cycle_spanning (from the\nchapter), and the reachability, via exists_walk_length_le_two.lemma Walk.isHamiltonian_of_isMaximumPath [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    [G.LocallyFinite] (h : G.IsOreGraph)\n    (hn : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V) {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v}\n    (hmax : p.IsMaximumPath) : p.IsHamiltonian := by\n  have h2 : 2 ≤ p.length := h.two_le_length_of_isMaximumPath hn hmax\n  -- Build a cycle through exactly the vertices of p.\n  obtain ⟨c, hcyc, hclen, hcsupp⟩ :=\n    hmax.isMaximalPath.exists_cycle_spanning h2 (h u v)\n  refine hmax.isHamiltonian_of_isCycle (fun w => ?_) hcyc hclen hcsupp\n  obtain ⟨q, -⟩ := h.exists_walk_length_le_two u w\n  exact q.reachable\n36. Give a direct proof of Ore's theorem, not using the closure: take a\nmaximum-length path; it is Hamiltonian of length ≥ 2, so\nIsHamiltonian.exists_isHamiltonianCycle_of_isOrePair closes it into a Hamiltonian\ncycle.example [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [G.LocallyFinite] (h : G.IsOreGraph)\n    (hn : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V) : G.IsHamiltonian := by\n  intro\n  have : Nonempty V := Fintype.card_pos_iff.mp (hn.trans_lt' (by decide))\n  -- A maximum-length path p is Hamiltonian and has length ≥ 2, so it closes up\n  -- into a Hamiltonian cycle.\n  obtain ⟨u, v, p, hmax⟩ := exists_isMaximumPath G\n  have hham := p.isHamiltonian_of_isMaximumPath h hn hmax\n  exact ⟨u, hham.exists_isHamiltonianCycle_of_isOrePair\n    (h.two_le_length_of_isMaximumPath hn hmax) (h u v)⟩\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks","header":"8.9. Exercises","id":"/Walks/#section-exercises-walks"}});